1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,127
In August 2017, at a steam fair
in Dorset, a six-and-a-half

2
00:00:05,207 --> 00:00:08,530
tonne steamroller drove
over a computer hard drive.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,250
Then they put what was left of it
through a steam-powered stone crusher.

4
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:16,590
The drive belonged to Terry Pratchett.

5
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:19,230
It held up to ten unfinished novels.

6
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:21,980
Nobody will ever read them.

7
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:27,340
This wasn't vandalism. It was
exactly what Pratchett wanted.

8
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:30,340
He had left instructions to do it.

9
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:32,500
But look at who actually did it.

10
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:35,010
Pratchett was already dead.

11
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:37,370
Somebody else started the engine.

12
00:00:39,817 --> 00:00:43,847
George R. R. Martin has been writing
The Winds of Winter for fifteen years.

13
00:00:44,737 --> 00:00:48,267
He has said that if he dies first,
the book simply won't be finished.

14
00:00:49,347 --> 00:00:50,897
That sounds like an instruction.

15
00:00:51,127 --> 00:00:54,437
But an instruction needs
somebody to carry it out.

16
00:00:55,487 --> 00:00:57,317
And that somebody won't be him.

17
00:00:58,897 --> 00:01:02,837
Martin writes on a DOS
machine running WordStar 4.0.

18
00:01:03,477 --> 00:01:04,927
He keeps two computers.

19
00:01:05,177 --> 00:01:06,587
One for email.

20
00:01:06,837 --> 00:01:09,267
One that only writes.

21
00:01:09,737 --> 00:01:13,287
He has said there are around eleven
hundred pages of the book on it.

22
00:01:14,037 --> 00:01:15,937
That part isn't a rumour.

23
00:01:16,197 --> 00:01:18,327
He has repeated it publicly for years.

24
00:01:19,397 --> 00:01:24,027
What nobody outside that room knows
is everything else. Older drafts.

25
00:01:24,177 --> 00:01:25,907
Chapters with two different endings.

26
00:01:26,337 --> 00:01:28,547
Scenes he wrote and then abandoned.

27
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:33,227
Notes about where a character ends
up, three hundred pages later.

28
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:37,017
So the first question isn't
who finishes the book.

29
00:01:38,157 --> 00:01:40,347
Who decides what happens to the files?

30
00:01:43,417 --> 00:01:45,037
That question has been answered before.

31
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:52,387
Franz Kafka left instructions with his
friend Max Brod. Burn everything. Unread.

32
00:01:53,187 --> 00:01:54,477
Brod didn't.

33
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:59,050
The Trial exists because of
that. So does The Castle.

34
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,730
Every novel Kafka wrote is
a disobeyed instruction.

35
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:06,590
Brod had told him to his
face that he would refuse.

36
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:09,490
Kafka left him the papers anyway.

37
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:12,030
Nobody asks the manuscript.

38
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:14,330
They ask whoever is holding it.

39
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:20,430
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

40
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,670
It really helps the channel.

41
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:26,340
Martin has already answered
part of this question.

42
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:28,980
He answered it in 1992.

43
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:33,780
That year a truck left Santa Fe
carrying his papers to Texas.

44
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:37,940
Some of the earliest pages of
A Game of Thrones were in it.

45
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:42,590
Still on dot matrix paper, with
the perforated edges attached.

46
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:49,000
Today that archive runs to over two
hundred boxes. Manuscripts. Letters.

47
00:02:49,110 --> 00:02:51,460
Notes. Board games. Replica swords.

48
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:55,250
Most of it is open to
any researcher who asks.

49
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:58,130
His private correspondence is closed.

50
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:01,320
And the rule for the closed
part is written down.

51
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,390
To see it, you have to present
a letter of permission.

52
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,010
A letter from Martin.

53
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:12,070
It is a rule that will one
day be impossible to satisfy.

54
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:15,010
The door doesn't lock when he dies.

55
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:18,560
It just stops having anyone behind it.

56
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:26,310
There is a third answer, and it has
been used before. Publish the mess.

57
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:31,470
Christopher Tolkien spent decades
editing his father's unfinished papers.

58
00:03:31,550 --> 00:03:33,840
Not into a finished novel.

59
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:36,080
Into twelve volumes of drafts.

60
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:41,990
Versions that contradict each other.
Notes in the margin. Dead ends.

61
00:03:42,881 --> 00:03:46,631
Vladimir Nabokov asked for his
last manuscript to be destroyed.

62
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:51,371
His son published it instead, as
photographs of the index cards.

63
00:03:52,571 --> 00:03:53,941
You can see the handwriting.

64
00:03:54,071 --> 00:03:55,731
You can see the crossings-out.

65
00:03:56,151 --> 00:03:58,421
Nobody there pretended to be the author.

66
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:01,021
They published what was on the page.

67
00:04:01,911 --> 00:04:03,661
Now picture that in Texas.

68
00:04:04,691 --> 00:04:06,851
A historian opens a
box and finds something

69
00:04:06,931 --> 00:04:09,601
labelled The Winds of Winter, draft seven.

70
00:04:10,329 --> 00:04:12,279
A chapter with three different endings.

71
00:04:13,289 --> 00:04:15,799
A note in the margin
saying: this doesn't work.

72
00:04:16,689 --> 00:04:18,689
Another one saying: try again.

73
00:04:18,769 --> 00:04:21,839
And a later version that
contradicts them both.

74
00:04:22,699 --> 00:04:24,609
For a historian, that's a treasure.

75
00:04:25,379 --> 00:04:28,689
For anyone who just wants to know
how the story ends, it's torture.

76
00:04:31,609 --> 00:04:34,619
And then there is the
fourth answer. Finish it.

77
00:04:35,753 --> 00:04:39,063
When Martin is asked about this,
he reaches for one comparison.

78
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:41,433
The Mystery of Edwin Drood.

79
00:04:42,592 --> 00:04:46,662
Charles Dickens died in 1870,
halfway through a murder mystery.

80
00:04:47,512 --> 00:04:49,622
He had told his biographer how it ends.

81
00:04:50,192 --> 00:04:51,822
He had not written it down.

82
00:04:52,742 --> 00:04:55,752
Three years later, a printer
in Vermont finished it.

83
00:04:56,302 --> 00:04:58,132
He said Dickens had asked him to.

84
00:04:59,142 --> 00:05:00,342
At a seance.

85
00:05:01,095 --> 00:05:03,905
A note appeared, signed
with Dickens's name.

86
00:05:04,675 --> 00:05:06,155
The printer said he wrote the rest of the

87
00:05:06,235 --> 00:05:09,145
book word for word,
exactly as it came to him.

88
00:05:10,715 --> 00:05:14,025
He included a preface that
Dickens had supposedly dictated.

89
00:05:15,375 --> 00:05:19,125
Decades later, Arthur Conan Doyle
went through it hunting for the fake.

90
00:05:19,935 --> 00:05:23,565
He checked the spelling. He
checked it for Americanisms.

91
00:05:24,615 --> 00:05:25,925
He found almost none.

92
00:05:27,645 --> 00:05:29,555
The manner was right, he decided.

93
00:05:30,325 --> 00:05:31,815
The trick of thought was right.

94
00:05:32,505 --> 00:05:35,075
It just sounded, he said,
like Dickens gone flat.

95
00:05:35,895 --> 00:05:37,325
But look at the shape of it.

96
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:41,905
A reconstruction that passes every
test anyone knows how to run.

97
00:05:42,395 --> 00:05:45,185
And underneath it, a story
nobody would call Dickens.

98
00:05:46,345 --> 00:05:48,295
That was 1873.

99
00:05:51,193 --> 00:05:53,903
Now imagine the same thing
with much better tools.

100
00:05:54,753 --> 00:05:57,823
Software could read every
draft on that DOS machine.

101
00:05:58,013 --> 00:06:02,363
Line them up. Date them.
Find the contradictions.

102
00:06:03,513 --> 00:06:06,103
And work out which ending
he was moving towards.

103
00:06:07,283 --> 00:06:08,873
It wouldn't produce one book.

104
00:06:10,003 --> 00:06:13,293
Change how you read one vague note
and you get a different ending.

105
00:06:14,123 --> 00:06:16,733
Trust the later drafts
more and you get another.

106
00:06:17,743 --> 00:06:20,533
You could generate hundreds
of them. Thousands.

107
00:06:21,679 --> 00:06:25,429
So which one is the real one? Version 37?

108
00:06:26,399 --> 00:06:27,789
Version 814?

109
00:06:28,739 --> 00:06:30,489
And there would be pressure to pick one.

110
00:06:31,039 --> 00:06:32,589
Publishers would want it.

111
00:06:32,719 --> 00:06:34,419
Fans would want it.

112
00:06:34,499 --> 00:06:38,309
The people who own the rights
would have every reason to want it.

113
00:06:39,299 --> 00:06:43,749
And somewhere in the world there would
be a writer saying... I can finish this.

114
00:06:46,109 --> 00:06:48,839
There's one more detail that
makes all of this worse.

115
00:06:49,609 --> 00:06:51,379
We already know part of the ending.

116
00:06:52,319 --> 00:06:55,739
In 2013, Martin sat
down with the television

117
00:06:55,819 --> 00:06:58,089
producers and told them how it ends.

118
00:06:59,269 --> 00:07:02,219
Not the whole thing. A
handful of destinations.

119
00:07:03,349 --> 00:07:06,369
What happens to Hodor.
What happens to Shireen.

120
00:07:06,449 --> 00:07:08,519
That Bran ends up on the throne.

121
00:07:09,379 --> 00:07:11,109
Think of the story as a map.

122
00:07:11,779 --> 00:07:14,669
He gave them some of the
places it has to arrive at.

123
00:07:15,099 --> 00:07:16,649
He didn't give them the road.

124
00:07:17,459 --> 00:07:21,609
The series had to build its own. It
cut characters. It merged storylines.

125
00:07:22,824 --> 00:07:25,084
Which means a reconstruction
could hit every single

126
00:07:25,164 --> 00:07:28,654
destination and still be a
completely different book.

127
00:07:31,664 --> 00:07:35,054
Because there is one thing no
amount of material can replace.

128
00:07:36,274 --> 00:07:39,264
A living author can throw
away three hundred pages.

129
00:07:40,034 --> 00:07:41,884
He can decide a character survives.

130
00:07:42,854 --> 00:07:44,564
He can rewrite the whole ending.

131
00:07:45,514 --> 00:07:50,124
He can look at your reconstruction and
say... No. That isn't what I meant.

132
00:07:51,074 --> 00:07:52,664
That is the real difference.

133
00:07:53,034 --> 00:07:54,604
Not that the book is unfinished.

134
00:07:55,684 --> 00:07:57,674
That the author can still say no.

135
00:07:59,574 --> 00:08:02,544
Once he's gone, the manuscript
stops being instructions.

136
00:08:03,884 --> 00:08:05,094
It becomes evidence.

137
00:08:06,674 --> 00:08:08,674
Evidence of what he was trying to do.

138
00:08:08,754 --> 00:08:11,324
Not a description of what he'd have done.

139
00:08:14,584 --> 00:08:17,474
There is one last answer,
and it's the quietest of all.

140
00:08:18,244 --> 00:08:22,314
The estate does exactly what he
asked. Nothing gets finished.

141
00:08:22,944 --> 00:08:24,194
Nothing gets published.

142
00:08:25,194 --> 00:08:28,764
The files are kept, and sealed.
It's his work. He gets to say.

143
00:08:29,557 --> 00:08:31,047
But look at it once more.

144
00:08:31,737 --> 00:08:33,887
Even that is somebody else's decision.

145
00:08:36,497 --> 00:08:39,447
Pratchett's estate said yes,
and started the steamroller.

146
00:08:40,747 --> 00:08:43,237
Brod said no, and that
is why we have Kafka.

147
00:08:44,397 --> 00:08:47,307
Christopher Tolkien published
the contradictions too.

148
00:08:48,337 --> 00:08:50,847
The printer in Vermont didn't ask anybody.

149
00:08:53,347 --> 00:08:55,117
Martin has said what he wants.

150
00:08:56,027 --> 00:08:58,577
But saying what you want is
not the same as deciding.

151
00:08:59,547 --> 00:09:04,677
The files might not survive him. The
archive will. So will the theories.

152
00:09:05,767 --> 00:09:07,677
He can still have the last word.

153
00:09:08,187 --> 00:09:09,797
He just has to finish it himself.

154
00:09:11,957 --> 00:09:14,807
Otherwise it belongs to
whoever opens the archive

155
00:09:16,186 --> 00:09:18,616
and asks for a letter
that nobody can write.
